{
  // Gate CI on HIGH and CRITICAL severity advisories only.
  //
  // WHY NOT all severities: `npm audit` reports advisories against the entire
  // dependency graph regardless of reachability. Classify moderate/low
  // advisories using these policy categories, with evidence for each decision:
  //   1. PHANTOM — a reported package is not the copy the application executes.
  //      Historical example: monaco-editor used its vendored DOMPurify instead
  //      of npm dompurify. The npm entry is now RESOLVED: PR #200 removed it
  //      when monaco-editor moved from 0.56.0 to 0.53.0.
  //   2. NOT WORTH THE FIX — transitive advisories that a root `overrides` entry
  //      COULD reach, but only by forcing a major version outside the range
  //      supported by its parent. Root overrides DO reach nested transitives of
  //      workspace dependencies (proven 2026-07-27, PR #150 -- an earlier note
  //      here claiming otherwise was wrong), so "no fix exists" is almost never
  //      the real reason. State the actual cost/benefit instead.
  //   3. UNREACHABLE — the vulnerable code path is not imported by anything this
  //      repo actually uses (historical example: @hono/node-server below).
  //
  // Gating on all severities produced constant red CI that blocked unrelated PRs
  // and trains force-merging -- a worse security posture than green CI that fails
  // only on what is actually severe and actionable. High/critical advisories are
  // rare, warrant breaking the build, and are usually fixable.
  // Current-tree snapshot (2026-11-11): the allowlist is empty.
  // Run `npm audit` for current findings.
  //
  // Current moderate advisory note (informational)
  //   GHSA-g3hv-75wg-m249 remains in the dev-only TypeDoc chain. TypeDoc
  //   0.28.20 requires minimatch ^10.2.5 and calls Minimatch.matchOne. A
  //   global minimatch 11 override breaks docs:api because v11 makes that
  //   method private. The full audit reports minimatch, typedoc, and
  //   typedoc-plugin-markdown. The production audit has no moderate findings.
  //   Revisit when TypeDoc supports minimatch 11 and build:web passes with it.
  //
  // RESOLVED moderate/low advisory notes (informational; not allowlist entries):
  //   - qs: GHSA-x5fp-wj9c-mxmx, GHSA-4mjr-xmp4-gh2g and GHSA-q8mj-m7cp-5q26.
  //     RESOLVED -- examples/run-repo-server's express@4.22.3 pins qs ~6.16.0.
  //     The root override from PR #210 was removed 2026-10-06; the lockfile
  //     retains a single qs 6.16.x copy without an override.
  //   - dompurify (via monaco-editor) was accepted as PHANTOM, class 1 above;
  //     now RESOLVED -- npm dompurify was removed by PR #200.
  //   - @opentelemetry/core GHSA-8988-4f7v-96qf was accepted as class 2 and
  //     resolved in PR #153. PR #294 bumped Lighthouse to 13.6.0, which
  //     retains its dev-only Node error reporting SDK (^11.8.0, locked to 11.11.0).
  //     The lockfile no longer contains @opentelemetry/core.
  //   - @hono/node-server GHSA-frvp-7c67-39w9 (<2.0.5, serve-static path
  //     traversal) was accepted as UNREACHABLE, class 3; now RESOLVED --
  //     PR #181 moved 1.19.14 to 2.1.1 on 2026-08-24, outside the affected range.
  //
  // If a HIGH/critical advisory ever lands that is itself phantom/unfixable,
  // allowlist that ONE advisory here by GHSA id with a dated reason -- a rare,
  // deliberate exception, not a standing treadmill.
  "high": true,
  // RESOLVED -- GHSA-vfj7-8cjw-p6xm was withdrawn upstream on 2026-10-23.
  // The entry was removed on 2026-11-11. braces 3.0.3 remains dev-only via
  // @changesets/cli -> @changesets/config -> micromatch and
  // eslint-config-next -> @next/eslint-plugin-next -> fast-glob -> micromatch.
  "allowlist": []
}
